  "not_isolable": {
    "note": "A doorless passage crosses the boundary of each of these, so none can be sealed on its own. Storage and C3 can only be sealed together (see the 'storage' target); Living Quarters and the Observation Deck likewise (see 'residential').",
    "areas": {
      "STO": "C3 Service Corridor",
      "C3": "Storage",
      "OBS": "Living Quarters",
      "LQ": "Observation Deck"
    }
  },
  "topology_notes": {
    "hb3_bypass": "Hangar Bay 3 touches both C2 (D8) and C3 (D9), so the door graph contains a cycle. Sealing the service sector therefore needs D7 AND D9 — a player who only remembers the obvious junction door leaves the back route open and fails the obligation.",
    "eng_hb4_adjacency": "Engineering shares a wall with Hangar Bay 4 (D11). Isolating Engineering alone needs D10 and D11; isolating the pair as one block needs D10 and D12 instead.",
    "remaining_passages": "Only two doorless passages remain: Observation Deck<->Living Quarters and Storage<->C3."
  },
  "door_numbering": "Internal doors are numbered top to bottom in horizontal bands, then left to right within a band. Every area with two doors therefore has consecutive numbers (Hangar Bay 3: D8/D9, Engineering: D10/D11, Hangar Bay 4: D11/D12) and C1 owns exactly D1-D5. Hangar doors H1-H5 match their bay number.",
  "isolation_vocabulary_note": "The fiction word for a multi-area volume is always 'sector'. Single rooms, bays and corridors keep their own names. Note that construction_sector is nested inside service_sector but needs MORE doors, because excluding Engineering and Hangar Bay 4 turns their corridor doors into boundary doors.",
  "label_nudge_note": "Optional per-door label offset in grid cells. Only for hand-tuning a label that collides with an area name; it moves the text, never the door.",
  "isolation_target_classes": {
    "sector": "A volume spanning more than one area. Not obvious from looking at the map — which doors bound it has to be learned. These are the ones that get a printed reference card.",
    "room": "A single room. One boundary, obvious on sight.",
    "bay": "A single hangar bay. Obvious on sight.",
    "corridor": "A single corridor. Obvious on sight, though the cut is wide."
  },
  "area_prose_note": "`name` and `sub` are the two label lines the canvas draws. `prose` is how an actor says the place out loud, and it is what the validator matches message text against. Where an area is also an isolation target, the two phrases agree."
}
